### Describe the bug

`BeforeConnect`/`BeforeNewClient` inject the OTel `CommandMonitor` into `mongo.Connect`/`mongo.NewClient` calls by looping over the variadic `opts ...*options.ClientOptions` and calling `opt.SetMonitor(monitor)` on any struct whose own `Monitor` field is `nil`:

```go
// instrumentation/go.mongodb.org/mongo-driver/mongo/client_hook.go
// (identical logic in BeforeConnect and BeforeNewClient)
for _, opt := range opts {
    if opt == nil {
        continue
    }
    if opt.Monitor == nil {
        opt.SetMonitor(monitor)
    }
}
```

This checks each `*ClientOptions` struct in isolation, but `mongo.NewClient`/`mongo.Connect` don't use the structs in isolation — they call `options.MergeClientOptions(opts...)`, which folds the whole slice into one struct by taking the **last non-nil** value of each field, in slice order:

```go
// go.mongodb.org/mongo-driver/mongo/options/clientoptions.go (MergeClientOptions)
if opt.Monitor != nil {
    c.Monitor = opt.Monitor
}
```

So if the caller passes multiple `*ClientOptions` and their own `CommandMonitor` is set on one that is **not the last struct in the slice**, the hook injects the OTel monitor into a later, still-`nil` struct — and since that struct comes later, the merge picks the OTel monitor and silently discards the user's own `CommandMonitor`. This is a realistic pattern: the driver's own doc comment on `NewClient` says "later Set* methods will overwrite the values from previous Set* method invocations", which explicitly encourages composing options across multiple structs (e.g. a shared base-options builder plus a per-call URI/override struct).

The bug is present in both driver versions instrumented by this repo (identical loop in both files):
- `instrumentation/go.mongodb.org/mongo-driver/mongo/client_hook.go` (`BeforeConnect`, `BeforeNewClient`)
- `instrumentation/go.mongodb.org/mongo-driver/v2/mongo/client_hook.go` (same pattern)

The existing test suite doesn't cover this: `client_hook_test.go` only asserts the single-struct case (monitor on the *only* struct is left untouched) and the both-nil case (both structs get injected). It never exercises "user monitor set on an earlier struct, later struct has none" — exactly the ordering `MergeClientOptions` treats specially.

### Environment

- OS: any (bug is in Go logic, not OS-specific)
- Go Version: 1.23+
- mongo-driver Version: v1.17.7 (also applies to the v2 driver instrumentation)

### To Reproduce

Standalone repro against the real `go.mongodb.org/mongo-driver v1.17.7` package (not a mock), replicating the hook's exact injection loop and then running the driver's real `options.MergeClientOptions`:

```go
func injectMonitorLikeHook(opts []*options.ClientOptions, otelMonitor *event.CommandMonitor) []*options.ClientOptions {
	for _, opt := range opts {
		if opt == nil {
			continue
		}
		if opt.Monitor == nil {
			opt.SetMonitor(otelMonitor)
		}
	}
	return opts
}

func TestUserMonitorInEarlierOptionsIsSilentlyDropped(t *testing.T) {
	userMonitor := &event.CommandMonitor{}
	otelMonitor := &event.CommandMonitor{}

	base := options.Client().SetMonitor(userMonitor)
	uriOpts := options.Client().ApplyURI("mongodb://localhost:27017")

	opts := []*options.ClientOptions{base, uriOpts}
	opts = injectMonitorLikeHook(opts, otelMonitor)

	merged := options.MergeClientOptions(opts...)

	if merged.Monitor != userMonitor {
		t.Fatalf("expected user's CommandMonitor to win, got otel monitor overriding it (merged.Monitor == otelMonitor: %v)", merged.Monitor == otelMonitor)
	}
}
```

Result:

```
=== RUN   TestUserMonitorInEarlierOptionsIsSilentlyDropped
    repro_test.go:40: expected user's CommandMonitor to win, got otel monitor overriding it (merged.Monitor == otelMonitor: true)
--- FAIL: TestUserMonitorInEarlierOptionsIsSilentlyDropped (0.00s)
```

Steps against the actual instrumentation:
1. Call `mongo.Connect(ctx, options.Client().SetMonitor(yourMonitor), options.Client().ApplyURI(uri))` in a project built with `otelc`.
2. `BeforeConnect` injects the OTel monitor into the second (`ApplyURI`) struct because its `Monitor` field is `nil`.
3. `mongo.NewClient` merges `opts` and keeps the *last* non-nil `Monitor` — the injected OTel one — discarding `yourMonitor`.

### Expected behavior

Injecting the OTel `CommandMonitor` should never cause an explicitly user-configured `CommandMonitor` to be dropped from the effective (post-merge) options, regardless of how many `*ClientOptions` structs are passed or which one carries the user's monitor. A compile-time instrumentation tool should be a safe, additive drop-in — it should not silently delete a user's pre-existing observability hook with no error or log.

### Additional context

Root cause: the hook decides whether to inject per-struct (`opt.Monitor == nil`), but the driver decides the *effective* value across the whole slice with last-non-nil-wins semantics. These two decision rules disagree whenever the user's `Monitor` isn't on the last struct in `opts`.

Suggested fix direction: don't mutate individual structs based on their own nil-check. Instead, determine the effective merged `Monitor` first (`options.MergeClientOptions(opts...).Monitor`). If it's `nil` (no user monitor anywhere in the slice), append a new trailing `options.Client().SetMonitor(monitor)` to `opts` rather than mutating an existing struct — because it's last, `MergeClientOptions`'s last-non-nil-wins rule guarantees it always takes effect precisely when needed, and it can never be overridden by (or override) any struct already in the slice, since a fresh `options.Client()` leaves every other field `nil`. Apply the same fix to both `mongo-driver/mongo` and `mongo-driver/v2/mongo` instrumentation, and add regression tests for the ordering case above alongside the existing single-struct/both-nil cases.

Happy to send a PR for this if assigned.

